    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, the visual presentation of melanoma can vary significantly across different racial groups due to differences in skin pigmentation. In patients with lighter skin tones, melanoma often presents as a new or changing pigmented lesion with asymmetric shape, irregular borders, and color variation. However, in darker-skinned individuals, melanomas frequently arise on acral sites (palms, soles, and nail beds), including subungual melanoma where nail changes like darkening, streaks, or splitting are the primary clues. Additionally, patients with darker skin may present with less obvious dermal metastatic tumors or nodules that appear erythematous, indurated, or bruise-colored rather than black, making earlier detection challenging.",
